## REASONING

**Decompose.** The request has one primary mechanistic goal — quantify how strongly soil texture controls sub-surface drainage (ELM QDRAI) across the agricultural landscape — and an implied validation goal. These are separable.

**Drivers & feasibility.** The only candidate driver named is soil texture, which is exactly the lever ELM single-column runs pull well: soil hydraulic properties (texture → porosity, Ksat, retention) directly govern the partitioning between drainage, surface runoff, and ET. This is feasible. Elevation range is modest (160 m) and no elevation-driven forcing downscaling is requested or supported, so I hold forcing uniform and do NOT claim to resolve any elevation/climate effect — a texture-stratified column set under uniform forcing isolates texture, which is precisely the question. Good alignment.

**The critical honesty point — validation.** Sub-surface drainage in this tile-drained agricultural landscape is physically realized largely through artificial tile drains, and the brief states tile-drain flow data are MISSING in-domain. So the primary target variable (drainage) has NO co-located observation. USGS wells are available but sparse — they constrain water-table depth, an indirect and imperfect proxy for drainage in a tile-drained system (ELM does not represent tile drains explicitly). I therefore flag drainage validation as a gap, offer the sparse wells only as a weak indirect/aggregated check, and set the verdict to **partial**: the mechanistic sensitivity of drainage to texture IS answerable; the observational validation of that drainage is NOT, in-domain.

**N.** Purely mechanistic across one driver: sample the dominant soil texture classes actually present (span coarse→fine), with light replication to expose within-class spread from secondary properties. ~5 texture strata × ~2 replicates ≈ 10 exploratory columns. Validation columns are pinned to well locations (count unknown, data-density limited).

**Placement.** Concentrate exploratory columns to span the soil-texture gradient across the agricultural footprint; place any validation columns at/near the sparse USGS wells.

